#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d702975-166e-5067-ae3c-1152fade8248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "edd4d491-c483-5004-b30f-68cf0ef76f43" :question/math-academy-id "e-5922" :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 18\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = x^(^)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(x, x^(^)) &= ‖x - x^(^)‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - x^(^)_{1})^{2} + (x_{2} - x^(^)_{2})^{2} + (x_{3} - x^(^)_{3})^{2} + (x_{4} - x^(^)_{4})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-3 - (-3))^{2} + (5 - 5)^{2} + (-1 - 1)^{2} + (3 - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0 + 0 + 4 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "04cb9ecc-68fa-546e-aa96-8a1ee050bbed" :question/math-academy-id "e-5923" :question/problem "Given the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - 2R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$, and $x_3$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = 2x_2-3x_3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x_{2} - 3x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nis a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we let $A$ be a matrix whose columns equal the elements of $\\mathcal B$, and we find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}x \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184055 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1224e2dd-96bc-5c24-aacf-3c36a7c3a1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "98aed663-0f72-5494-9a5f-ba2fdf94764a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9906" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}27 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 27\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{72}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$. Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$. So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= (0 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\cdot 0)/(\\sqrt{0^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}2\\right)= \\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle, as desired."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bacbba68-575d-54c1-ac50-26655dbcc218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183192} {:answer/id #uuid "120a11ff-a55d-53a5-aad4-2d019bed5b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183193} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9455c5-0d3a-5a00-9e27-680db6e7653e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183194} {:answer/id #uuid "5af9c2d4-b83f-51c4-bfb3-81e50f5e96b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183195} {:answer/id #uuid "7996d4a5-d6d0-5556-a8f1-c0801c6829b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a9455c5-0d3a-5a00-9e27-680db6e7653e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183194} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9af6694-8e3e-50e8-b6eb-4d9eb02cc1e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183191}] :question/id #uuid "27e616d4-1e96-59b7-b91d-2a687a44a3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-39858" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}, {x_{1} - 3x_{2} + 2x_{3} = 0; 3x_{1} - 9x_{2} + 6x_{3} = 0$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d702975-166e-5067-ae3c-1152fade8248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf303a11-ddde-5901-ab68-af90bef86cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183660} {:answer/id #uuid "09292a91-e6f0-56df-9eab-f96421dd7a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183661} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3a982b-2232-5b4f-a755-7fd3f60c96f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183662} {:answer/id #uuid "61d56700-f455-5ffd-8b16-823f8b072b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183663} {:answer/id #uuid "6769ee16-9439-532f-ab3e-1996fbc544ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf303a11-ddde-5901-ab68-af90bef86cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183660} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf1fb01-a5b2-512f-9bcb-b3352fa36a9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183659}] :question/id #uuid "16d7e659-66cb-54f0-b03c-983af77b46e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-83617" :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$, where\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$."}]}
